import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3];p=ROOT/'dist/cis304-content.json';d=json.loads(p.read_text())
v=[]
def term(name,meaning,why,wrong,source,exp=''):
 v.append(dict(id=f'304-v{len(v)+1:02}',term=name,exp=exp,answer=meaning,teach=why,correct=meaning,distractors=wrong.split('|'),source=source,section=d['questions'][source-1]['section'],q=f'What does {name} mean?'))
term('Data','Raw individual facts before interpretation','A list of sales amounts is data. Organizing them into a useful sales report produces information.','A reasoned decision about future action|The ability to apply experience|Only the rules followed by employees',1)
term('Information','Data processed or organized into meaning','The context matters: “30” is a fact; “30 orders sold today” has a useful meaning.','Uninterpreted facts only|Physical computing equipment|A judgment about which goal to pursue',1)
term('Knowledge','Understanding that lets you apply information','Knowing how sales patterns affect stock helps you use a report rather than simply read its numbers.','Any number stored in a database|A cable connecting computers|The money left after costs',1)
term('Wisdom','Judgment used to assess situations and choose sensible action','You consider consequences and trade-offs before deciding, rather than assuming all information points to one automatic answer.','Every raw fact collected|The storage space of a computer|A formatted report without a decision',1)
term('Hardware','Physical computing equipment','A scanner can be touched. Its program cannot: that program is software.','Instructions executed by computers|Agreed steps for doing work|Facts about a transaction',4)
term('Software','Instructions and programs run by computers','The program tells equipment what to do. It needs hardware to execute those instructions.','The physical processor itself|People operating a system|Only paper procedures',5)
term('RAM','Temporary working memory used by active programs','Work currently being used goes in RAM; saved files belong in persistent storage.','Permanent storage that keeps all files without power|A processor that executes instructions|An output screen',4,'Random Access Memory')
term('CPU','The processor that executes computer instructions','The CPU performs computation; RAM holds current work and storage keeps saved files.','The permanent storage drive|A networking cable|The display used to show results',4,'Central Processing Unit')
term('IS','People, processes, data, hardware, and software organized to handle information','An IS includes human work and procedures, not only a computer application.','Only hardware connected to a network|Only the data in one file|Only an organization’s long-term plan',2,'Information System')
term('DIKW','Data → Information → Knowledge → Wisdom','The model moves from facts toward understanding and judgment.','Data → Integration → Knowledge → Workflow|Design → Information → Knowledge → Work|Data → Information → Workflow → Knowledge',1,'Data Information Knowledge Wisdom')
term('Business strategy','The broad plan for goals and competitive advantage','It sets direction so process and technology choices serve business goals.','A record of one customer payment|A physical network layout alone|The daily steps of one transaction',7)
term('Business model','How a business creates, delivers, and captures value','It describes the offer, customers, and way of earning money.','Only who reports to whom|Only the computer system design|Only a measure of labor efficiency',9)
term('Cost leadership','Competing through lower costs','Lower operating costs can support competitive prices and sustainable margins.','Competing only through unique features|Setting high prices regardless of value|Increasing expenses to maximize input',7)
term('Differentiation','Competing through offerings customers value as distinct','A customer chooses a custom product or special service because the difference matters to them.','Making every offering identical to competitors|Competing solely through lower costs|Separating all department databases',7)
term('Stakeholder','A person or group affected by or interested in the work or result','Employees, customers, and managers may care about different outcomes in the same process.','Only the owner of computer hardware|Only the person writing software|A record of inventory levels',13)
term('DSRP','Distinctions, Systems, Relationships, Perspectives','Use it to examine boundaries, parts and wholes, interactions, and viewpoints.','Data, Systems, Resources, Processes|Decisions, Strategy, Relationships, Projects|Distinctions, Software, Results, Productivity',11,'Distinctions Systems Relationships Perspectives')
term('Input','A resource or item that goes into a process','An order, ingredient, labor hour, or piece of information can be an input.','Only the finished result|Only the final customer payment in every process|The comparison between actual and planned output',12)
term('Output','The result produced by a process','Outputs may be goods, completed services, information, or records.','Every resource consumed before work starts|Only a computer screen in all contexts|The steps followed to do the work',12)
term('Actor','A person or system performing an activity','A cashier and an automated payment system can both be actors in a process model.','Only an outside customer|A diagram’s arrow showing sequence|A resource that never performs an action',12)
term('Handoff','Transfer of work or responsibility between participants','When sales passes an order to the warehouse, the next team becomes responsible for its step.','A measurement of output per hour|An organization’s overall strategy|A duplicate copy that no other team receives',13)
term('As-is model','A model of the process as it currently works','You need to understand current work before identifying what to change.','The proposed future process|The hardware purchased next year|A list of goals with no activities',13)
term('To-be model','A model of the proposed future process','It shows the intended new way of doing work, which still needs implementation and evaluation.','The current process before changes|A record of historical transactions only|An employee reporting chart only',13)
term('UML','A standardized visual modeling language','Activity diagrams can show activities, decisions, and flow using shared symbols.','A financial measurement formula|An enterprise software vendor|A method that forbids diagrams',13,'Unified Modeling Language')
term('Project','A temporary effort creating a unique result','Developing a new ordering system ends; repeatedly processing orders continues.','An indefinitely repeated operational routine|A permanent department by definition|A collection of hardware with no objective',14)
term('Procurement','Obtaining needed goods or services from suppliers','The organization is the buyer: it requests resources, receives them, and pays.','Selling goods to a customer|Transforming raw materials into finished products|Preparing financial statements only',18)
term('Production','Transforming resources into goods or completed services','For a salon, doing a haircut is production of a service; it need not be a factory.','Buying all required resources from suppliers|Recording only financial transactions|Collecting a customer’s payment without doing the work',18)
term('Fulfillment','Meeting a customer order by delivering goods or services and handling payment','The organization is the seller. Its work follows the customer order toward completion.','Obtaining supplies as a buyer|Only recording financial statements|Only recruiting employees',18)
term('Accounting','Recording, checking, and reporting financial activity','Sales and purchasing create financial events; accounting turns those events into reliable records and reports.','Only making physical products|Only negotiating supplier orders|Only delivering the customer’s order',18)
term('Purchase requisition','An internal request to obtain goods or services','An employee asks the organization to authorize a purchase before it orders from a vendor.','An order sent to a selected external vendor|A bill sent by a vendor|A record of a customer sale',19)
term('Purchase order','A buyer’s order sent to a selected vendor','The buyer asks the vendor to supply specified goods or services; an internal request is a requisition.','An internal request before supplier ordering|The vendor’s request for payment|The seller’s financial statement',19)
term('Sales order','A record managing the seller’s handling of a customer order','The seller tracks what was ordered and what must be supplied. It is not the same as asking the customer to pay.','An employee’s internal purchase request|A vendor bill received by the buyer|A final statement of all business profit',19)
term('Invoice','A document requesting payment for supplied goods or services','The seller sends a sales invoice; the buyer receives that document as a vendor invoice. An invoice is not automatically proof that payment occurred.','An internal request for purchasing approval|Only a list of employees|A guarantee that the customer has already paid',19)
term('PI','Initial planned input','This is the resource amount expected before doing the work, such as a budget of ten hours.','Actual input used|Initial planned output|Actual output achieved',15,'Planned Input')
term('AI','Actual input used','In these formulas AI means actual input, not artificial intelligence. It is what the work really consumed.','Actual output achieved|Initial planned input|Initial planned output',15,'Actual Input')
term('PO','Initial planned output','This is the target result, such as 100 acceptable orders.','Actual output achieved|Initial planned input|Actual input used',16,'Planned Output')
term('AO','Actual output achieved','This is the measured result, such as 90 acceptable orders completed.','Actual input used|Initial planned output|Initial planned input',16,'Actual Output')
term('Efficiency','Planned input divided by actual input (PI / AI)','The course compares resource use with the initial plan. Keep the units comparable.','Actual output divided by planned output|Actual output divided by actual input|Actual input divided by actual output',15)
term('Effectiveness','Actual output divided by planned output (AO / PO)','It compares the achieved result with the target, rather than comparing resource use.','Planned input divided by actual input|Actual output divided by actual input|Planned output divided by actual input',16)
term('Productivity','Actual output per actual input (AO / AI)','Orders per labor hour is productivity. Compare the same units across time.','Planned input divided by actual input|Actual output divided by planned output|Actual input divided by planned output',17)
term('Cycle time','Elapsed time from a process’s start to its finish','Waiting between steps can lengthen cycle time even if individual tasks are quick.','Only the output quantity|Only the cost of purchased software|The total number of departments',20)
term('Bottleneck','A step that limits or slows the overall flow of work','A slow approval can make orders wait even if every other step is fast.','Any unused computer regardless of impact|The final customer output|A shared objective agreed by all teams',21)
term('BPM','Ongoing management of business processes','It includes running work, measuring results, and deciding how to improve it.','Only small incremental changes|Only radical process replacement|Only financial record keeping',21,'Business Process Management')
term('BPI','Incremental improvement of existing business processes','Small changes often preserve much of the current way of working.','Fundamental redesign from scratch|Only observing work without changing it|Eliminating all business processes',22,'Business Process Improvement')
term('BPR','Fundamental rethinking and radical redesign of processes','It challenges the design itself to seek large performance gains.','Small repeated tweaks to the current design|A routine to back up computer files|The same as buying any new application',23,'Business Process Re-engineering')
term('Enterprise','An organization or group of organizations with shared goals','The term can cover one company or several collaborating organizations.','Only a very large mainframe computer|Only the finance department|Any collection of unrelated customers',27)
term('EA','The overall blueprint connecting business and technology to goals','It includes business, data, applications, and technology rather than treating IT as isolated purchases.','Only the hardware installed in one room|Only the daily accounting process|Only the organization’s advertising plan',27,'Enterprise Architecture')
term('Framework','A structured way to organize and develop an architecture','It guides which views and concepts to consider; the architecture is the organization’s resulting design.','The installed hardware itself|A finished design identical for every organization|The data in one customer order',27)
term('Governance','Rules and responsibilities for making and overseeing decisions','It clarifies who decides and what standards they follow.','The speed of a processor|Only hiring new employees|A list of finished products',29)
term('SOA','An architecture built from reusable software services','A service does a defined job and can be used by multiple applications through an agreed interface.','A rule that all software must be cloud-hosted|A design that isolates every application|A single central computer with no service reuse',32,'Service-Oriented Architecture')
term('IT infrastructure','The actual technology resources and services supporting IT operations','The design specifies needs; infrastructure provides the working technology.','The business’s goals alone|Only the department reporting structure|An abstract blueprint with no implemented resources',33,'Information Technology infrastructure')
term('Platform','An environment on which applications or services run','Applications depend on the platform’s hardware/software capabilities.','A record of a customer purchase|A business goal without technical capabilities|The entire enterprise strategy',35)
term('Functional area','A specialized category of business work, often a department','Sales, HR, and accounting are functional areas; an order can cross several areas.','An individual physical computer component|A formula for measuring outputs|A complete enterprise-wide process in every case',38)
term('HR','The function responsible for people-related work such as hiring and training','HR means human resources. Its work concerns employees, not purchasing ingredients.','The function that records only supplier bills|The function that produces network hardware|The function that designs every customer invoice',39,'Human Resources')
term('Silo effect','Isolation that prioritizes department goals over shared organizational goals','A team can finish its own task while the whole customer order still fails. Shared measures and information help.','Healthy specialization with good coordination|A shared database used by all departments|A coordinated process crossing several departments',40)
term('Standardization','Agreeing on consistent procedures, formats, or definitions','Two branches can use the same form even before their systems are connected.','Connecting systems and sharing work only|Keeping all methods unrelated|Radical redesign of every business goal',42)
term('Integration','Connecting parts so they share information and work together','A sale updating both inventory and accounting connects work across functions.','Using identical forms but never sharing them|Separating each function’s records|Replacing all workers with software',43)
term('FAIS','An information system focused on one functional area','Payroll for HR illustrates functional scope; enterprise scope involves connecting multiple areas.','A system defined by company-wide integration|An operating system for a computer|An intercompany shipping contract',44,'Functional Area Information System')
term('EIS','An information system connecting enterprise functions and processes','In this course the E is Enterprise. ERP belongs within this broader category.','An isolated functional-area app by definition|Only executive dashboards in this course|Only a network connecting computers',45,'Enterprise Information System')
term('ERP','Integrated modules and shared data supporting core enterprise processes','Different departments can use the same connected transaction record.','Only production scheduling in a spreadsheet|Unconnected software for each department|Only a supplier’s public website',47,'Enterprise Resource Planning')
term('Module','A part of an ERP system supporting a particular business area','Finance and procurement modules have different jobs but can share data.','A completely unrelated system that cannot connect|A company’s long-term strategy|An individual row of customer data',47)
term('Database','An organized collection of stored data','The ERP’s shared data provides consistent records for several functions; accuracy still depends on good inputs.','Only a computer’s screen|Only a list of business goals|Only the staff working in accounting',47)
term('Data migration','Moving existing data into a new system','Incorrect conversions or missing records can affect many ERP users, so migration must be checked.','Writing a new competitive strategy|Training staff without moving records|Only buying a network cable',49)
term('Interorganizational','Across the boundaries of different organizations','A buyer and its supplier coordinating electronically are working between organizations.','Only within one department|Only within one organization|Only within a single computer',51)
term('Intraorganizational','Within one organization','Connecting sales and accounting inside one company is intraorganizational.','Across two independent companies|Only between supplier and customer companies|Only across national borders',51)
